/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Examples Examples
 * @brief Runnable demonstrations of DspCore APIs.
 *
 * Examples mirror the source hierarchy and share portable code between the
 * Arduino sketch and native desktop adapter:
 *
 * @code
 * examples/<module>/<example>/
 * |-- CMakeLists.txt
 * |-- <example>.ino
 * |-- Shared.h
 * |-- Shared.cpp
 * `-- Desktop/
 *     |-- CMakeLists.txt
 *     `-- main.cpp
 * @endcode
 *
 * Available examples:
 *
 * | Target | Shows |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `DspCore_Core_Version` | DspCore and Foundation versions |
 * | `DspCore_LUT_Line` | Reserve, normalized samples, shrink/regrowth |
 * | `DspCore_LUT_Surface` | Row-major 2D access, bounds and reshape |
 * | `DspCore_LUT_Volume` | 3D access, moves, overflow and release |
 * | `DspCore_Interpolation_Linear` | Physical coordinates, extrapolation and invalid spans |
 * | `DspCore_Interpolation_Lookup` | Closed/periodic boundaries and 2D/3D lookup |
 * | `DspCore_Generators_Periodic` | Waveform reference samples and phase conventions |
 * | `DspCore_Generators_Shaping` | Normalized sigmoid endpoints and gamma morph |
 * | `DspCore_Generators_Envelopes` | Stable tiny curves and exponential/logarithmic reference values |
 * | `DspCore_Generators_LUTEnvelope` | Normalized table mapping and constant singleton shapes |
 * | `DspCore_Generators_Noise` | State continuity between blocks and zero-seed rejection |
 * | `DspCore_Generators_Windows` | Symmetric Hann/Hamming coefficients and singleton windows |
 * | `DspCore_Filters_FIR` | 33-tap moving-average low-pass: 300 Hz + 6 kHz signal |
 * | `DspCore_Filters_Biquad` | 12 kHz low/high-pass response to a 100 Hz--20 kHz sweep |
 * | `DspCore_Filters_IIR` | Generic numerator/denominator step-pulse response and a0 normalization |
 * | `DspCore_Analysis_Levels` | Block peak/RMS and overflow-safe scaling |
 * | `DspCore_Analysis_Envelope` | Rectification and distinct attack/release time constants |
 *
 * Build and run one on the desktop:
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --examples-on
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/DspCore_Core_Version_d
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Examples_DesktopTools Desktop output and plotting tools
 *
 * `examples/Tools/` contains host-side convenience tools for the native filter
 * demonstrations. It is not part of the DspCore library, is not compiled into
 * an Arduino sketch, and is not required by an embedded consumer:
 *
 * | File | Purpose |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `plot_signal.py` | Reads a signal CSV and writes a time-domain/spectrum PNG using NumPy and Matplotlib. |
 * | `plot_signal.m` | MATLAB equivalent of the Python plotter. |
 * | `render_signal.sh` | macOS/Linux wrapper that finds Python and runs `plot_signal.py`. |
 * | `render_signal.ps1` | Windows PowerShell equivalent of the shell wrapper. |
 *
 * `examples/Tools/` intentionally has no `CMakeLists.txt`: these are scripts,
 * not build targets. `examples/CMakeLists.txt` names them so that a native
 * `--examples-on` build copies them beside its executables and an exported
 * Release examples package installs them beside its launcher. A normal DspCore
 * library build without examples does not include or distribute these tools.
 *
 * The documentation integration has a separate purpose. `cmake/BuildDocs.cmake`
 * supplies `docs/Topics/Examples/Images/` as Doxygen's image search path, and
 * `docs/Doxyfile` consumes that value through `IMAGE_PATH`. Doxygen then copies
 * the versioned PNG snapshots into its HTML output; no Python or MATLAB is
 * needed merely to read the published documentation.
 *
 * The desktop filter examples write long, plot-ready CSV signals to
 * `~/Documents/DspCoreExampleOutput/` by default. Pass `--output <directory>` to choose
 * another location. Each CSV has `sample`, `time_seconds`, input and output
 * columns plus title/sample-rate metadata. Plot it with Python (NumPy and
 * Matplotlib) or MATLAB using the included helpers. The helpers are copied
 * beside native build executables and shipped beside exported examples:
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/DspCore_Filters_FIR_d --output ./output
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/render_signal.sh ./output/fir_moving_average.csv
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{.matlab}
 * plot_signal("output/fir_moving_average.csv")
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Examples_FilterPlots Filter example plots
 *
 * These versioned snapshots are generated from the desktop filter examples
 * with the included plotting helper. They show the time-domain signals above
 * and their normalized spectra below; regenerate the CSV data and PNGs when
 * changing the example signal or filter configuration.
 *
 * @subsection DspCore_Examples_FIRPlot FIR moving-average low-pass
 *
 * The 33-tap moving average retains the 300 Hz component while substantially
 * attenuating the 6 kHz component.
 *
 * \image html fir_moving_average.png "FIR moving-average example" width=900px
 *
 * @subsection DspCore_Examples_IIRPlot Generic IIR step-pulse response
 *
 * The generic IIR example makes its normalized a0=2 recurrence and its stateful
 * response to the step pulse directly inspectable.
 *
 * \image html iir_step_pulse.png "Generic IIR step-pulse example" width=900px
 *
 * @subsection DspCore_Examples_BiquadPlot Biquad low-pass and high-pass sweep
 *
 * A logarithmic sine sweep contrasts the 12 kHz low-pass and high-pass outputs.
 *
 * \image html biquad_sweep.png "Biquad low-pass and high-pass sweep example" width=900px
 *
 * Enable and export Release examples with:
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh --examples-on
 * @endcode
 */
